EARLY EARTH, NASA LAVA-WORLD ANALOGUE -- early-earth-nasa.png -- 2048 x 1024
Credit: NASA Visualization Technology Applications and Development (VTAD).
Texture reprojection and choice of early-Earth analogy: DEEPscope.
Official source: https://science.nasa.gov/resource/55-cancri-e-3d-model/
Model released 1 May 2019; download published 20 September 2023.
Download: https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/astro/exo-explore/2023/09/55_Cancri_e_1_24364.glb
Downloaded GLB SHA-256: e3f3381c4440adc1df37ee26bd54ea885969616412efa43ed461971fb8d2a8c0
Selected embedded base-colour image: image 1, LHS3844b_assembled.jpg (stored as PNG).
That is the internal image name in NASA's published 55 Cancri e GLB, not a
reidentification of the source model by DEEPscope.
Processing: tools/bake_nasa_globe_texture.py intersects radial directions with
the 3072 published mesh triangles and interpolates their original UV coordinates.
The 2048 x 1536 cube atlas is sampled bilinearly into a 2:1 longitude/latitude
map; glTF +Y becomes north, model +X is the map centre. All 2,097,152 samples
intersect the source mesh. The output has matching seam pixels and uniform
pole rows. No terrain generation, recolouring or generative AI is used.
Only base colour is retained; emission and metallic shading are not baked in.
This is NASA artwork of an exoplanet reused as a VISUAL ANALOGUE for a molten
Earth episode. It is NOT an Earth reconstruction made by NASA, a dated Hadean
map, or a claim about actual early-Earth geography or temperature. The UI label
and live credit explicitly identify the analogue and its original planet.
Reproduce with NumPy, SciPy and Pillow:
python3 tools/bake_nasa_globe_texture.py --source /path/to/55_Cancri_e_1_24364.glb --out public/assets/surfaces/early-earth-nasa.png
